{"id": "0d93533d-7ce2-48e8-9b9b-38f6f29736a4", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T11:09:33.314426", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "The FsLex parser is still failing. The issue might be with the `[<refinement>]` attribute on the token type, or the way I'm defining the rule. Let me look at actual FsLex examples more carefully.\n\nIn FsLex, the format is:\n```\n(* header *)\n%%\n(* rules *)\n```\n\nThe rules section uses `rule rulename = parse` syntax. The `[<refinement>]` attribute on a type is used for refinement types in FsLex, but it might not be needed here. Let me simplify and use a more standard approach.\n\nActually, looking at FsLexYacc documentation more carefully, the token type with `[<refinement>]` is used when you want FsLex to generate the token type for you. But since I'm defining it manually, I should not use `[<refinement>]`.\n\nAlso, the issue might be that FsLex doesn't like the `and` bindings for helper functions. Let me try a simpler approach - just define the tokenize rule directly without helper functions, using the built-in lexbuf methods.\n\nLet me look at a working example. The standard FsLex format for a simple lexer:\n\n```\nopen Parser\nopen FSharp.Text.Lexing\n\n%%\n\nrule tokenize = parse\n  | ' ' | '\\t' | '\\r' | '\\n' { tokenize lexbuf }\n  | '0'..'9' as c ->\n      let start = lexbuf.LexemeStart\n      let rec loop () =\n        match peekChar lexbuf with\n        | '0'..'9' -> ignore (nextChar lexbuf); loop ()\n        | _ -> ()\n      loop ()\n      INT (int (lexbuf.Lexeme))\n  | '+' { PLUS }\n  | '-' { MINUS }\n  | '*' { STAR }\n  | '/' { SLASH }\n  | '(' { LPAREN }\n  | ')' { RPAREN }\n  | eof { EOF }\n```\n\nWait, but `peekChar`, `nextChar`, `lexeme` are built-in FsLex functions that are available in the rule body. Let me try without the helper functions.\n\nActually, the real issue might be simpler. Let me check if the `[<refinement>]` attribute is causing the parse error. In FsLex, `[<refinement>]` is used to tell FsLex to generate the type definition. If I'm defining the type myself, I shouldn't use it.\n\nLet me try a completely clean approach without the refinement attribute and without helper functions.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "df5e77e2-70d0-45c6-85fe-de0ebf09e5aa", "tool_call": {"id": "df5e77e2-70d0-45c6-85fe-de0ebf09e5aa", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"The FsLex parser is still failing. The issue might be with the `[<refinement>]` attribute on the token type, or the way I'm defining the rule. Let me look at actual FsLex examples more carefully.\\n\\nIn FsLex, the format is:\\n```\\n(* header *)\\n%%\\n(* rules *)\\n```\\n\\nThe rules section uses `rule rulename = parse` syntax. The `[<refinement>]` attribute on a type is used for refinement types in FsLex, but it might not be needed here. Let me simplify and use a more standard approach.\\n\\nActually, looking at FsLexYacc documentation more carefully, the token type with `[<refinement>]` is used when you want FsLex to generate the token type for you. But since I'm defining it manually, I should not use `[<refinement>]`.\\n\\nAlso, the issue might be that FsLex doesn't like the `and` bindings for helper functions. Let me try a simpler approach - just define the tokenize rule directly without helper functions, using the built-in lexbuf methods.\\n\\nLet me look at a working example. The standard FsLex format for a simple lexer:\\n\\n```\\nopen Parser\\nopen FSharp.Text.Lexing\\n\\n%%\\n\\nrule tokenize = parse\\n  | ' ' | '\\\\t' | '\\\\r' | '\\\\n' { tokenize lexbuf }\\n  | '0'..'9' as c ->\\n      let start = lexbuf.LexemeStart\\n      let rec loop () =\\n        match peekChar lexbuf with\\n        | '0'..'9' -> ignore (nextChar lexbuf); loop ()\\n        | _ -> ()\\n      loop ()\\n      INT (int (lexbuf.Lexeme))\\n  | '+' { PLUS }\\n  | '-' { MINUS }\\n  | '*' { STAR }\\n  | '/' { SLASH }\\n  | '(' { LPAREN }\\n  | ')' { RPAREN }\\n  | eof { EOF }\\n```\\n\\nWait, but `peekChar`, `nextChar`, `lexeme` are built-in FsLex functions that are available in the rule body. Let me try without the helper functions.\\n\\nActually, the real issue might be simpler. Let me check if the `[<refinement>]` attribute is causing the parse error. In FsLex, `[<refinement>]` is used to tell FsLex to generate the type definition. If I'm defining the type myself, I shouldn't use it.\\n\\nLet me try a completely clean approach without the refinement attribute and without helper functions.\\n\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-d01af075-0d7c-4a01-a5b6-d17fec6ba388", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "think: {\"thought\": \"The FsLex parser is still failing. The issue might be with the `[<refinement>]` attribute on the token type, or the way I'm defining the rule. Let me look at actual FsLex examples more carefully.\\n\\nIn FsLex, the format is:\\n```\\n(* header *)\\n%%\\n(* rules *)\\n```\\n\\nThe rules section uses `rule rulename = parse` syntax. The `[<refinement>]` attribute on a type is used for refinement types in FsLex, but it might not be needed here. Let me simplify and use a more standard approach.\\n\\nActually, looking at FsLexYacc documentation more carefully, the token type with `[<refinement>]` is used when you want FsLex to generate the token type for you. But since I'm defining it manually, I should not use `[<refinement>]`.\\n\\nAlso, the issue might be that FsLex doesn't like the `and` bindings for helper functions. Let me try a simpler approach - just define the tokenize rule directly without helper functions, using the built-in lexbuf methods.\\n\\nLet me look at a working example. The standard FsLex format for a simple lexer:\\n\\n```\\nopen Parser\\nopen FSharp.Text.Lexing\\n\\n%%\\n\\nrule tokenize = parse\\n  | ' ' | '\\\\t' | '\\\\r' | '\\\\n' { tokenize lexbuf }\\n  | '0'..'9' as c ->\\n      let start = lexbuf.LexemeStart\\n      let rec loop () =\\n        match peekChar lexbuf with\\n        | '0'..'9' -> ignore (nextChar lexbuf); loop ()\\n        | _ -> ()\\n      loop ()\\n      INT (int (lexbuf.Lexeme))\\n  | '+' { PLUS }\\n  | '-' { MINUS }\\n  | '*' { STAR }\\n  | '/' { SLASH }\\n  | '(' { LPAREN }\\n  | ')' { RPAREN }\\n  | eof { EOF }\\n```\\n\\nWait, but `peekChar`, `nextChar`, `lexeme` are built-in FsLex functions that are available in the rule body. Let me try without the helper functions.\\n\\nActually, the real issue might be simpler. Let me check if the `[<refinement>]` attribute is causing the parse error. In FsLex, `[<refinement>]` is used to tell FsLex to generate the type definition. If I'm defining the type myself, I shouldn't use it.\\n\\nLet me try a completely clean approach without the refinement attribute and without helper functions.\\n\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
