 '\\r' | '\\n' { tokenize lexbuf }\n  ...\n```\n\nAnd define the token type separately. But then how do I reference the token type?\n\nActually, looking at FsLexYacc examples more carefully, I see that the token type is typically defined using `[<refinement>]` in the header. Let me try putting the type definition first, then the opens:\n\n```\n[<refinement>]\ntype token =\n  | INT of int\n  | PLUS\n  | MINUS\n  | STAR\n  | SLASH\n  | LPAREN\n  | RPAREN\n  | EOF\nopen Parser\nopen FSharp.Text.Lexing\n%%\nrule tokenize = parse\n  ...\n```\n\nHmm, but I tried that too. Let me try without any header at all and see if FsLex can generate a default token type.\n\nActually, I think the real issue might be that FsLex doesn't support `open` in the header. Let me try putting the opens in the rules section instead, or using fully qualified names.\n\nWait, let me re-read the FsLex documentation. The header section is F# code that gets included verbatim in the output file. The `%%` separates the header from the rules. The rules section is parsed by FsLex.\n\nSo the issue is that FsLex's parser is failing on the header. But the header is just F# code - why would FsLex fail to parse it?\n\nUnless... FsLex doesn't actually parse the header as F# code. It just copies it verbatim. The `%%` is the only thing FsLex's parser needs to understand.\n\nLet me try with an empty header:\n\n```\n%%\nrule tokenize = parse\n  | ' ' | '\\t' | '\\r' | '\\n' { tokenize lexbuf }\n  | '0'..'9' as c ->\n      let rec loop () =\n        match peekChar lexbuf with\n        | '0'..'9' -> ignore (nextChar lexbuf); loop ()\n        | _ -> ()\n      loop ()\n      INT (int (lexeme lexbuf))\n  | '+' { PLUS }\n  | '-' { MINUS }\n  | '*' { STAR }\n  | '/' { SLASH }\n  | '(' { LPAREN }\n  | ')' { RPAREN }\n  | eof { EOF }\n```\n\nAnd then add the opens and type definition in the rules section as F# code.\n\nActually, I just realized something. Looking at the FsLex source code, the header section is parsed as F# 