{"id": "22d24ca7-1a17-430c-beb2-4e2467897731", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T11:10:26.550391", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "Still failing. The `%%` is not being recognized as a section separator. Let me look at the FsLex source code to understand the expected format.\n\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc GitHub repo more carefully, I see that FsLex uses a different format. The file format is:\n\n```\n(* F# code *)\nrule rulename = parse\n  | pattern { action }\n  ...\n```\n\nThere's NO `%%` separator in FsLex! The `%%` separator is used in Menhir/ocamllex, not FsLex.\n\nIn FsLex, the file is parsed as a sequence of F# code and rule definitions. The `rule` keyword starts a rule definition. Everything before the first `rule` is considered header code.\n\nSo the format should be:\n\n```\nopen Parser\nopen FSharp.Text.Lexing\n\nrule tokenize = parse\n  | ' ' | '\\t' | '\\r' | '\\n' { tokenize lexbuf }\n  | '0'..'9' as c ->\n      let rec loop () =\n        match peekChar lexbuf with\n        | '0'..'9' -> ignore (nextChar lexbuf); loop ()\n        | _ -> ()\n      loop ()\n      INT (int (lexeme lexbuf))\n  | '+' { PLUS }\n  | '-' { MINUS }\n  | '*' { STAR }\n  | '/' { SLASH }\n  | '(' { LPAREN }\n  | ')' { RPAREN }\n  | eof { EOF }\n```\n\nNo `%%` at all! Let me try this.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "f419ae4c-417e-4164-973d-861340397c1f", "tool_call": {"id": "f419ae4c-417e-4164-973d-861340397c1f", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"Still failing. The `%%` is not being recognized as a section separator. Let me look at the FsLex source code to understand the expected format.\\n\\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc GitHub repo more carefully, I see that FsLex uses a different format. The file format is:\\n\\n```\\n(* F# code *)\\nrule rulename = parse\\n  | pattern { action }\\n  ...\\n```\\n\\nThere's NO `%%` separator in FsLex! The `%%` separator is used in Menhir/ocamllex, not FsLex.\\n\\nIn FsLex, the file is parsed as a sequence of F# code and rule definitions. The `rule` keyword starts a rule definition. Everything before the first `rule` is considered header code.\\n\\nSo the format should be:\\n\\n```\\nopen Parser\\nopen FSharp.Text.Lexing\\n\\nrule tokenize = parse\\n  | ' ' | '\\\\t' | '\\\\r' | '\\\\n' { tokenize lexbuf }\\n  | '0'..'9' as c ->\\n      let rec loop () =\\n        match peekChar lexbuf with\\n        | '0'..'9' -> ignore (nextChar lexbuf); loop ()\\n        | _ -> ()\\n      loop ()\\n      INT (int (lexeme lexbuf))\\n  | '+' { PLUS }\\n  | '-' { MINUS }\\n  | '*' { STAR }\\n  | '/' { SLASH }\\n  | '(' { LPAREN }\\n  | ')' { RPAREN }\\n  | eof { EOF }\\n```\\n\\nNo `%%` at all! Let me try this.\\n\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-1d550f1e-e9a6-4196-93f8-b18a16bfb8db", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "think: {\"thought\": \"Still failing. The `%%` is not being recognized as a section separator. Let me look at the FsLex source code to understand the expected format.\\n\\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc GitHub repo more carefully, I see that FsLex uses a different format. The file format is:\\n\\n```\\n(* F# code *)\\nrule rulename = parse\\n  | pattern { action }\\n  ...\\n```\\n\\nThere's NO `%%` separator in FsLex! The `%%` separator is used in Menhir/ocamllex, not FsLex.\\n\\nIn FsLex, the file is parsed as a sequence of F# code and rule definitions. The `rule` keyword starts a rule definition. Everything before the first `rule` is considered header code.\\n\\nSo the format should be:\\n\\n```\\nopen Parser\\nopen FSharp.Text.Lexing\\n\\nrule tokenize = parse\\n  | ' ' | '\\\\t' | '\\\\r' | '\\\\n' { tokenize lexbuf }\\n  | '0'..'9' as c ->\\n      let rec loop () =\\n        match peekChar lexbuf with\\n        | '0'..'9' -> ignore (nextChar lexbuf); loop ()\\n        | _ -> ()\\n      loop ()\\n      INT (int (lexeme lexbuf))\\n  | '+' { PLUS }\\n  | '-' { MINUS }\\n  | '*' { STAR }\\n  | '/' { SLASH }\\n  | '(' { LPAREN }\\n  | ')' { RPAREN }\\n  | eof { EOF }\\n```\\n\\nNo `%%` at all! Let me try this.\\n\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
