{"id": "d6c9d12e-0b98-418b-bd14-b88f8eacde9c", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T11:12:33.108305", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "Now I can see the FsLex format clearly. Looking at FsLex's own lexer source (`fslexlex.fsl`), I can see:\n\n1. The header is enclosed in `{ ... }` (curly braces), NOT `%%`\n2. The header contains F# code (module declaration, open statements, helper functions)\n3. After the `}` closing brace, the rules follow directly with `rule token = parse`\n\nSo the format is:\n```\n{\n(* header code *)\nmodule MyModule\nopen Parser\nopen FSharp.Text.Lexing\n}\n\nrule tokenize = parse\n  | ...\n```\n\nThis is the key insight! FsLex uses `{ ... }` for the header, not `%%`. The `%%` separator is used in ocamllex/FsYacc, not FsLex.\n\nLet me rewrite Lexer.fsl with this format.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "fd9299a3-05b6-4e90-8b58-e9efcf544b97", "tool_call": {"id": "fd9299a3-05b6-4e90-8b58-e9efcf544b97", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"Now I can see the FsLex format clearly. Looking at FsLex's own lexer source (`fslexlex.fsl`), I can see:\\n\\n1. The header is enclosed in `{ ... }` (curly braces), NOT `%%`\\n2. The header contains F# code (module declaration, open statements, helper functions)\\n3. After the `}` closing brace, the rules follow directly with `rule token = parse`\\n\\nSo the format is:\\n```\\n{\\n(* header code *)\\nmodule MyModule\\nopen Parser\\nopen FSharp.Text.Lexing\\n}\\n\\nrule tokenize = parse\\n  | ...\\n```\\n\\nThis is the key insight! FsLex uses `{ ... }` for the header, not `%%`. The `%%` separator is used in ocamllex/FsYacc, not FsLex.\\n\\nLet me rewrite Lexer.fsl with this format.\\n\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-4fb1740e-182c-49f6-bfb6-7e1e897c0dbd", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "think: {\"thought\": \"Now I can see the FsLex format clearly. Looking at FsLex's own lexer source (`fslexlex.fsl`), I can see:\\n\\n1. The header is enclosed in `{ ... }` (curly braces), NOT `%%`\\n2. The header contains F# code (module declaration, open statements, helper functions)\\n3. After the `}` closing brace, the rules follow directly with `rule token = parse`\\n\\nSo the format is:\\n```\\n{\\n(* header code *)\\nmodule MyModule\\nopen Parser\\nopen FSharp.Text.Lexing\\n}\\n\\nrule tokenize = parse\\n  | ...\\n```\\n\\nThis is the key insight! FsLex uses `{ ... }` for the header, not `%%`. The `%%` separator is used in ocamllex/FsYacc, not FsLex.\\n\\nLet me rewrite Lexer.fsl with this format.\\n\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
