{"id": "df69f346-055b-4426-9c18-738345f9d317", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T11:13:39.275150", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "Now I understand the FsLex syntax. Looking at the parser grammar:\n\n1. A clause is `regexp CODE` where CODE is the F# action code in `{ ... }`\n2. The regexp syntax is:\n   - `CHAR` for single characters like `'+'`\n   - `STRING` for strings like `\"hello\"`\n   - `IDENT` for macros\n   - `regexp regexp` for sequence\n   - `regexp PLUS` for one or more\n   - `regexp STAR` for zero or more\n   - `regexp BAR regexp` for alternation\n   - `LPAREN regexp RPAREN` for grouping\n   - `LBRACK charset RBRACK` for character classes\n\nSo the issue is that `'0'..'9'` is a character range syntax that should work. But the `as c ->` syntax is NOT part of FsLex syntax. FsLex doesn't support `as` bindings in the way I was using them.\n\nLooking at the FsLex lexer source (fslexlex.fsl), I see it uses:\n```\n| ident_start_char ident_char* { IDENT (lexeme lexbuf) }\n```\n\nThe `lexeme` function is defined in the header and returns the matched string. So I should use:\n```\n| '0'..'9'+ { INT (int (lexeme lexbuf)) }\n```\n\nWait, but `'0'..'9'` is a character range. Let me check if FsLex supports this syntax. Looking at the parser, I see `CHAR DASH CHAR` in the charset rule, which means character ranges like `'0'..'9'` should work.\n\nBut the issue is that `'0'..'9'+` might be parsed as `'0'..'9'` followed by `+` (the PLUS token). Let me check...\n\nActually, looking at the FsLex parser more carefully, I see that `PLUS` is a token, not an operator. The `regexp PLUS` rule means `regexp` followed by the `PLUS` token. So `'0'..'9'+` would be parsed as `'0'..'9'` followed by `+` (the PLUS token), which would be a sequence.\n\nWait, but in the FsLex lexer source, I see:\n```\n| ident_start_char ident_char* { IDENT (lexeme lexbuf) }\n```\n\nHere `ident_char*` uses `*` as a postfix operator (regexp_star). So `+` should also work as a postfix operator.\n\nBut looking at the parser, I see:\n```\n| regexp PLUS %prec regexp_plus  { Seq[$1;Star $1] }\n```\n\nThis means `regexp PLUS` is parsed as a sequence of `regexp` followed by the `PLUS` token, NOT as `regexp` followed by the `+` postfix operator.\n\nWait, that doesn't make sense. Let me re-read the parser...\n\nActually, looking at the parser more carefully:\n- `PLUS` is a token (the `+` character)\n- `regexp PLUS` is a rule that matches `regexp` followed by the `PLUS` token\n- The semantic action `Seq[$1;Star $1]` means \"one or more of the regexp\"\n\nSo `PLUS` is used as a postfix operator in FsLex syntax! Similarly, `STAR` is used as a postfix operator.\n\nSo `'0'..'9'+` should be parsed as `'0'..'9'` followed by `PLUS`, which means \"one or more digits\".\n\nBut wait, the issue is that `'0'..'9'` is a character range, and `+` is the PLUS token. So `'0'..'9'+` should be parsed as a sequence of `'0'..'9'` followed by `PLUS`, which means \"one or more digits\".\n\nHmm, but the error is on line 8, column 7, which is the `as c ->` part. Let me look at the FsLex parser again...\n\nLooking at the parser, I see that a clause is `regexp CODE`. The CODE is the F# action code in `{ ... }`. So the syntax is:\n```\n| regexp { action_code }\n```\n\nThe `as c ->` syntax is NOT part of FsLex syntax. FsLex doesn't support `as` bindings.\n\nSo I need to rewrite the lexer to use `lexeme` instead of `as c ->`.\n\nLet me try:\n```\n| '0'..'9'+ { INT (int (lexeme lexbuf)) }\n```\n\nWait, but `lexeme` returns a string, and `int` can parse a string to an int. Let me check if this works.\n\nActually, looking at the FsLex source code, I see that `lexeme` is defined as:\n```fsharp\nlet lexeme (lexbuf : LexBuffer<char>) = new System.String(lexbuf.Lexeme)\n```\n\nSo `lexeme lexbuf` returns a `string`, and `int` can parse a string to an int.\n\nLet me try this approach.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "too